## Pathology of head and neck neoplasms

- **Head and neck region** - contains:
  - Upper aerodigestive tract - nasal cavity, nasalpharynx, paranasal sinuses, oral cavity, oralpharynx, pharynx, hypopharynx, larynx and cervical esophagus
  - Major and minor salivary glands
  - Thyroid
  - Associated LN, soft tissues and bones
- **Commonest H&N neoplasm** - squamous cell carcinoma and its precursors and variants
- **Principles of Mx:**
  - **DIagnosis** - through Bx (core needle, incisional or excisional Bx), or cytology (FHA)
  - **Tumour excision w/ adequate surgical margins** - goal of Mx in most cases of invasive SCC:
    - <u>Surgical margin goals</u> - guidelines provided by NCCN:
      - Complete resection of the tumor means the inked specimen margins are negative in situ invasive tumour
      - Margin adequacy criteria varies by tumour site
      - Clear margins are defined as at least 5mm of clearance from invasive tumour or carcinoma in situ
      - Close margins are defined as 2-5 mm of clearance depending on the specific subsite
    - <u>Appropriate to margin assessment</u> - two approaches:
      - Specimen margins - surgical margins identified in the resection specimen
      - Tumour bed margins - removal of surgical margins from the surgical defect by the surgeon and sent separately for evaluation
- **Overview of H&N neoplasms:**
  - SCC percursors
  - SCC
  - Salivary gland tumours
- **Squanous cell carcinoma precursors:**
  - <u>Leukoplakia, erythroplakia and leukoerythroplakia</u> - clinical entities reflecting oral squamous proliferation appearing as white, red or speckled mucosal plaques
  - <u>Squamous hyperplasia</u> - a reactive phenomenon of thickened squamous epithelium due to a variety of causes ranging from ill-fitting dentures to toxic substances
  - <u>Squamous dysplasia</u> - premaligant proliferation of squamous epithelium affecting the mucosal surface of the upper aerodigestive tract, a/w a number of non-modifiable and modfiable risk factors, grade dependent on atypia, architecture, ad maturation
  - <u>Proliferative verrucous leukoplakia</u> - important precursor lesion of SCC often found in gingiva and alveolar mucosa w/ higher risk of malignant transformation than simple leukoplakia
- **Squamous cell carcinoma:**
  - **Carcinogenesis and risk factors of SCC** - SCC in the head and neck evolves along 3 major pathways:
    - <u>Tobacco and alcohol use</u> (most common) - tumors are typically driven by widespread chromosomal instability, with mutations involving TP53 and deletion of CDKN2A
    - <u>Human papillomavirus</u> - high risk serotypes (e.g. HPV 16) typically causing SCC of the oropharynx (80%) and a/w earlier nodal metastasis, occuring in younger patients and are a/w oral sex
    - <u>Ebstein-Barr virus</u> - accounts for tumours of the nasopharynx in endemic regions, and to a lesser-extent non-endemic regions, and may also play a role in SCC of the salivary glands, typically having undifferentiated morphology with minimal keratinization and an aboundant lymphoid stroma ('lymphoepithelial tissue')
  - **Pathological features** - invasive SCC defined by invasion through the basement membrane, but ultimate evidence of squamous differentiation includes:
    - <u>Keratinisation</u> - represented by pearls of dense keratin within tumor nest found focally or more generally within the SCC
    - <u>Intercellular bridges</u> - tenacious tight junctions in between malignant cells
    - <u>p63, 40 expression on IHC</u> - role in diagnosis of poorly differentiated SCC where associated dysplaisa is not seen and intercellular bridge and frank keratinization are absent
  - **Prognostic factors of SCC:**
    - <u>Stage</u> - most important prognostic factor, principally determined by tumor size and nodal metastasis, but different TNM staging are developed for different primaries of the H&N
    - <u>Histological grade</u> - histological grading through assessment of cytological atypia, morphology and tissue architecture, though prognostic value of histologic grading is unclear in a/w survival (grade more a/w malignant transformation)
    - <u>Tumour depth of invasion</u> - incorporated in TNM staging as the risk of nodal metastasis in oral SCC is independent of tumour size
    - <u>Perineural and lymphovascular invasion</u> - a/w increased risk of local or distant recurrence
    - <u>Extracapsular extension</u> - extracapsular extension of tumor from LN into surrounding fibroconnective tissue is a -ve prognostic factor, especially in non-HPV associated tumour (often an indication for adjuvant chemoradiation)
    - <u>Surgical margin status</u> - inadequate margins is a/w increased risk of local recurrence
    - <u>Pattern of invasion</u> - pattern of invasion demonstrating discontinuous groups of small cells or individual cells a/w poorer prognosis in some prognostic models for oral cavity
  - **Histological variants of SCC:**
    - Verrucous carcinoma
    - Basaloid SCC
    - Spindle cell SCC
- **Other mucous epithelial tumour** - exhaustive list:
  - Schneiderian papilloma - exophytic, inverted, oncocytic
  - Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis
  - Sinonasal undifferentiated carcinoma
  - Sinonasal adenocarcinoma

## Overview of the diagnosis and staging of head and neck cancer

- **Clinical suspicion of head and neck cancer** - H&N malignancies are suspected if patients present w/ one or more of the following Sx if otherwise unexplained:
  - <u>Otalgia</u> - cranial nerves V, VII, IX, X contribute to sensory innervation of EAC and middle ear, which may be affected in head and neck neoplasm
  - <u>Neck mass</u> - presentation w/ LN involvement should raise suspicion of head and neck malignancy, especially if chronic, persistent, firm, fixed, matted or a/w reduced mobility
  - <u>Nasal congestion or epistaxis</u> - especially if chronic, and ipsilateral nasal obstruction
  - <u>Odynophagia or dysphagia</u> - suggestive of disease of oropharynx, and esophagus
  - <u>Mass or ulceration</u> - growing mass or ulcerations in the mouth, skin, tonsils, salivary glands or thyroid
  - <u>Hoarsensess</u> - patient presenting for hoarseness should be evaluated of CA larynx especially if Hx is compatible
- **Principles of clinical features of H&N cancers:**
  - <u>Features of local tumour based on anatomical location</u> - tumours presenting w/ specific anatomical locaiton will have a distinct set of presenting symptoms
  - <u>Features of nodal metastasis</u> - presenting with lymphadenopathy or a growing neck mass
  - <u>Features of distant metastasis</u> - dependent on site of metastasis (e.g. bone pain, jaundice)
- **Local Features of nasal cavity tumours:**
  - Nasal obstruction
  - Epistaxis
- **Local Features of nasopharyngeal tumours:**
  - Nasal congestion and epistaxis
  - Unilateral conductive hearing loss
  - Tinnitus
  - Pain
- **Local Features of oral cavity tumours:**
  - Persistent mouth pain or non-healing mouth ulcers
  - Dysphagia or odynophagia
  - Bleeding
  - Referred otalgia
  - Dysarthria - if tongue cancer advanced resulting in invasion of tongue
- **Local Features of oropharyngeal tumours:**
  - Dysphagia and odynophagia
  - Otalgia
  - OSA
  - Bleeding
- **Local Features of hypopharyngeal tumour:**
  - Dysphagia and odynophagia
  - Otalgia
  - Haemoptysis
- **Local Features of laryngeal cancer:**
  - Hoarseness
  - Haemoptysis
  - Stridor
- **Local Features of salivary gland tumours:**
  - Neck mass
  - Facial nerve palsy

